ment and should should uphold most  democratically enacted economic  regulations the progressive regulations  of the time so what is a conservative  like Roberts doing invoking this  Progressive Era hero homes in the  Obamacare case well the answer is that  these men both are proponents or were in  Holmes's case proponents of the  philosophy of judicial restraint or  judicial deference and this is the idea  that the supreme court and the courts  are the least democratic branch of our  government and as a result they owe  extra respect or deference to the  elected branches to the democratically  accountable branches you know the  president elected by the people Congress  elected by the people state lawmakers  legislators elected by the people of  those states and so when a  constitutional question comes before the  Supreme Court dealing one of one of  these democratically enacted statutes  the judges should put the thumb on the  scale on the side of the government on  the side of lawmakers then and tip the  balance that way so this is this idea of  judicial deference and although it  starts off as a philosophy of the  progressives and Holmes is writing  during the Progressive air and the  progressives love him he is a hero to  the progressives he's hugely influential  in the New Deal when the New Deal  triumphs of the Supreme Court nineteen  thirty-eight thirty-seven thirty-eight  Holmes's views are vindicated are are  accepted by the majority and they're  still with us today but judicial  restraint or deference is also become a  touchstone of modern conservatives in  the last forty years the conservative  legal movement has has rallied around  this call in many ways and and Robert  Bork late Robert Bork who was Ronald  Reagan of course nominated to the  Supreme Court and was a federal judge a  very distinguished conservative legal  scholar fork was a proponent of this  Oliver Wendell Holmes style judicial  deference and in his book the tempting  of America Bork wrot