adhere to the  doctrine of judicial restraint which  allows state legislatures broad latitude  in making economic policy decisions in  their respective jurisdictions so  there's the ghost of Oliver Wendell  Holmes you know dead a century still  haunting haunting these decisions and  you know I think it's it's safe to say  that that judicial deference in that  case failed failed these property owners  but it failed the Constitution also and  and it serves as as an example of these  these two philosophies in action that  does the Constitution require the courts  to defend defend liberty and to look  really look at what the government is  doing doesn't mean you overturn every  government action but you really look at  what's going on rather than just say  well we're going to leave it to the  lawmakers to decide if it's a public you  so we're going to trust them we're going  to put the thumb on the scale on the on  the government side rather than look is  there pretextual reasons is there is  there something else going on here and  that really is you know the libertarian  view is that's the role of the courts  that's the courts job you know John  Roberts and Obamacare said it's not our  job to protect the people from the  consequences of their political choices  and the libertarian view is it is your  job to protect  people from the consequences their  political choices when it when those  choices violate the Constitution 51% of  the voters don't you know uh just a pure  majority does not make an  unconstitutional action permissible so  that's the libertarian legal view in my  book as I said traces this debate and  looks at this and focuses on the  libertarian work that's been done and  I'll just I'll just conclude now by  saying that you know what what what this  debate really is is a fundamental  question about the nature of our system  and then sort of asks us a fundamental  question we know what sort of system  what's constitutional system do we want  to have do we want to live under you  know should the Supreme Court defer to  the will of the majority and and and  respect the will of majority and  therefore uphold most democratically  enacted laws or does the Constitution  empower the courts to act as a check on  the other branches of government and  then to act and act aggressively and act  in even an anti-democratic fashion when  when the majority has has overstepped  its boundaries and so that's I think  that is a that we can look at the  history of the Supreme Court and we can  see this debate coming up time and time  again it's its present in our current  debates the Obamacare was heard in  Supreme Court last week I was there for  that I'm sure people have questions  about that so I won't talk any more  about it this is a really fundamental  debate and it helps us understand what  the court has been up to in the past but  also helps us understand what's coming  down the line and I I think it's really  essential to understanding this this  third branch of our government and they  may be politicians but they have a lot  of power and these ideas these ideas  matter brokered thank you all very much  really enjoyed
 

